/**
\page reportsM Reports Menu

The <b>Reports</b> Menu shows commands that generate a read-only report about
the current layout (\ref mainW). Each report opens in its own window with
<b>Save...</b>, <b>Print...</b> and <b>Page Setup...</b> buttons, the same as
the Parts List (\ref cmdEnum) window.

- <b>Unconnected Endpoints</b> - displays the <b>Unconnected Endpoints
  Report</b> (\ref cmdReports) dialog, listing every track endpoint in the
  layout that isn't connected to another track.

- <b>Turnout Density</b> - displays the <b>Turnout Density Report</b>
  (\ref cmdReportsturnout) dialog, listing turnout counts and track length
  by layer, and flagging switching-heavy layers.

- \subpage cmdReports

- \subpage cmdReportsturnout

*/

/**
\page cmdReports Unconnected Endpoints Report

The <b>Unconnected Endpoints Report</b> command lists every track endpoint
in the entire layout that is not connected to another track -- one row per
open endpoint, giving its <b>Track</b> id, <b>X</b>/<b>Y</b> position,
<b>Angle</b>, and whether it's <b>Nearby</b> another open endpoint.

![](reportuncon.png)

A summary line above the list gives the total count of open endpoints and how
many are flagged <b>Nearby</b>, updated whenever the report is generated or
refreshed.

Unlike the Parts List (\ref cmdEnum) command, this report always covers the
whole layout; it does not consider the current selection (\ref cmdSelect).

A track with more than two endpoints (for example a turntable) can
contribute more than one row to the report -- one row per unconnected
endpoint on that track, not one row per track.

The <b>Nearby</b> column flags an endpoint when another open endpoint, on a
<i>different</i> track, lies within the layout's Connection Distance
preference (Options > \ref cmdPref) -- the same distance the editor
itself uses to decide whether two endpoints are close enough to connect.
A flagged pair usually means a missed connection rather than a deliberate
dead end. Flagged rows are listed first, grouped together; every row stays
in the list either way, nothing is filtered out.

Selecting a row in the list pans the <b>Main Canvas</b> (\ref mainW) to
that endpoint's position and draws a temporary open-circle indicator there,
so you can quickly find any listed endpoint on the layout. The indicator
is purely visual -- it's never saved with the layout and disappears as
soon as the report window closes. Its color can be changed on the
<b>Colors</b> (\ref cmdRgbcolor) dialog, under <b>Report Indicator</b>.

The <b>Refresh</b> button recomputes the report in place -- useful after
fixing a flagged endpoint elsewhere in the layout, without closing and
reopening the report window.

The <b>Save As...</b> button opens a <i>File Save</i> dialog window that's
used to name and save the report to a text file.

The <b>Print</b> button opens the <i>Print</i> dialog which is used to
print the report.

The <b>Done</b> button closes the <b>Unconnected Endpoints Report</b>
window.

*/

/**
\page cmdReportsturnout Turnout Density Report

The <b>Turnout Density Report</b> command lists, for each layer with any
track on it, the total track length in feet, the turnout count, and the
turnout density (turnouts per 100ft of track) -- one row per layer, sorted
by density, highest first. A layer whose density exceeds 10 turnouts per
100ft is flagged <b>HEAVY</b> (a common sign of a staging yard or other
switching-heavy area).

A summary line above the list gives the layout's total turnout count,
total track length, and overall density, updated whenever the report is
generated or refreshed.

Unlike the Parts List (\ref cmdEnum) command, this report always covers the
whole layout; it does not consider the current selection (\ref cmdSelect).

Any turnout with fewer than 3 endpoints (possibly broken or incomplete) is
listed separately in the <b>Save...</b>/<b>Print</b> output, under
<b>PARTIAL TURNOUTS</b>; the summary line notes when any exist. Unlike the
Unconnected Endpoints Report (\ref cmdReports), this report has no
click-to-navigate -- it's a layout-wide statistic, not a list of specific
locations to visit.

The <b>Refresh</b> button recomputes the report in place -- useful after
changing turnouts or track elsewhere in the layout, without closing and
reopening the report window.

The <b>Save As...</b> button opens a <i>File Save</i> dialog window that's
used to name and save the report to a text file.

The <b>Print</b> button opens the <i>Print</i> dialog which is used to
print the report.

The <b>Done</b> button closes the <b>Turnout Density Report</b> window.

*/
